Title: Diagnosis of test `test_getPackageName_Class`

Details:
The test case `test_getPackageName_Class` fails for the input `String[].class`. This means the method `ClassUtils.getPackageName(Class<?>)` does not correctly handle array class types like `String[].class`.

The source for `ClassUtils.getPackageName(Class<?>)` is:
```java
public static String getPackageName(Class<?> cls) {
    if (cls == null) {
        return StringUtils.EMPTY;
    }
    return getPackageName(cls.getName());
}
```
This method delegates to `getPackageName(String)` passing `cls.getName()`.

The method `getPackageName(String)` is:
```java
public static String getPackageName(String className) {
    if (className == null) {
        return StringUtils.EMPTY;
    }

    // Strip array encoding
    // Strip Object type encoding

    int i = className.lastIndexOf(PACKAGE_SEPARATOR_CHAR);
    if (i == -1) {
        return StringUtils.EMPTY;
    }
    return className.substring(0, i);
}
```
The bug is evident because the comments indicate it should strip array encoding and object type encoding from the class name string, but there is no implementation to do so. When `cls.getName()` is called on `String[].class`, the name returned is `[Ljava.lang.String;` (the internal JVM notation for array classes) which includes array type encoding characters `[` and `;`. The method `lastIndexOf(PACKAGE_SEPARATOR_CHAR)` finds the last '.' character but that will be part of the `[Ljava.lang.String;` string, not a straightforward package separator. Because of the leading array encoding `[L` and trailing `;` characters, the `lastIndexOf('.')` might still find the `.` in `"java.lang.String"`, but the returned substring is not the expected package name `"java.lang"`.

The failure likely resulted because the `getPackageName(String)` method should normalize or strip the array encoding notation prefix `[` and suffix `;` before searching for the package separator and extracting the package name. Since that logic is missing, the method returns either incorrect package name or empty string when input is an array class.

In conclusion:
- `getPackageName(Class<?>)` relies on `getPackageName(String)`
- `getPackageName(String)` has placeholder comments for "Strip array encoding" and "Strip Object type encoding" but no actual implementation
- When passed array class names in internal format (like `[Ljava.lang.String;`), it returns wrong package name
- The failing test used `String[].class` which exposes this bug

The fix should implement array encoding stripping in `getPackageName(String)` so it returns proper package name for array class names.

Suggested methods to fix:
- org.apache.commons.lang.ClassUtils.getPackageName(java.lang.String)
- org.apache.commons.lang.ClassUtils.getPackageName(java.lang.Class<?>)

